course: everyday
lesson: 382
title: Our street against theirs
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The Mill Lane kids had a war with Station Road, and nobody could remember how it started. «Our street against theirs!» Kieran shouted, and the first name everyone called was Maddie's. Someone painted «MILL LANE RULES» on the Station Road bus stop in dripping red, and the next week the Mill Lane kids pinched the Station Road football and wouldn't give it back. Sam was told to be the «lookout» for a street fight — and spent the whole evening feeling sick.
explain: But the lines kept getting blurry. Jake lives on Station Road but goes to school with the Mill Lane kids, so both sides called him a traitor. Maddie met a Station Road girl at swimming, and they got on so well they shared crisps every Saturday. Kieran's cousin lives on Station Road, and at Christmas they had to sit next to each other with paper hats on, pulling a cracker. Lily simply refused to join the «our street» gang: her best friend lives on Station Road.
explain: Then Sam asked Maddie the question nobody had asked: «Why do the streets even fight?» Maddie opened her mouth and shut it again. «Because... they're Station Road?» It was a reason that was really no reason at all.
explain: A street is just where your family happens to live. The kids on the other street go to the same shops, swim in the same pool, and have the same mums telling them to be home by six. Fighting over a road name gets people hurt, gets property damaged, and turns friends and cousins into «enemies» for nothing.
fact: Feuds between streets or groups often go on long after anyone remembers why they started.
fact: Painting words on a bus stop or wall without permission is graffiti, a kind of vandalism; the council pays to clean it, and that money comes from everyone.
fact: Taking someone's ball and refusing to give it back is stealing, even if it is called a «pinch» or a joke.
fact: A «lookout» for a fight is part of the fight; adults and police treat lookouts as joining in.
fact: People are not good or bad because of the street they live on.
fact: Friends, cousins and classmates often live on «the other side», so street wars hurt people's own families and friendships.
fact: Refusing to join a gang is allowed, and it is often the bravest choice in the group.
fact: Asking «why are we doing this?» can stop a feud more quickly than any fight.
fact: Saying no to being a lookout and telling a trusted adult is the safe choice.
fact: Things that belong to someone else, like a ball, should be given back, and saying sorry helps calm things down.
exercise: Why did the Mill Lane kids fight Station Road? | Nobody really had a reason except the street name | Station Road kids had stolen all their bikes last summer | A teacher had told them to stay apart | The two streets were playing a football match
exercise: Painting «MILL LANE RULES» on the bus stop is an example of what? | Graffiti, which damages other people's property | A harmless joke that washes off in the next rain | Street art that the council is glad to have | A way to make the bus come sooner
exercise: Who usually pays to clean graffiti off a bus stop? | The council, with money from everyone | Only the kids who live on Station Road | The bus driver, out of his own weekly wages | Nobody, it is just left there for ever
exercise: The Mill Lane kids kept the Station Road football. What is that? | Stealing, and they should give it back | A fair prize for winning | Borrowing, as long as they play with it | Fine, because it landed on their side
exercise: Why did Sam feel sick about being the lookout? | He knew a lookout is part of the fight | He was hungry and missed his tea | He did not like the dark | He wanted to be the one fighting instead
exercise: What was the wise thing for Sam to do when asked to be lookout? | Say no and tell a trusted adult | Agree, but keep his eyes shut the whole time | Agree and just watch from far away | Agree so nobody calls him chicken
exercise: Jake lives on Station Road but goes to school with Mill Lane kids. What does this show? | Street lines don't match real friendships | Jake is secretly a spy for Station Road | Jake should pick one side and stick to it | Jake should move house to the other street
exercise: Maddie gets on really well with a Station Road girl at swimming. What does that show? | Kids from the other street are just like you | Maddie is being disloyal to her own street | The girl is only pretending to be nice to her | Swimming makes enemies friendly with each other
exercise: Kieran had to sit with his Station Road cousin at Christmas. Why might that feel awkward? | The street war made a family member seem like an enemy | Cousins should never sit together at Christmas | His cousin had a bigger paper hat than he had | He did not like the noise of crackers
exercise: Why did Lily refuse to join the «our street» gang? | Her best friend lives on Station Road | She was scared of getting paint on her clothes | She secretly lives on Station Road herself | Her mum would not let her out after tea
exercise: Was Lily wrong to refuse? | No, saying no to a gang is allowed and brave | Yes, everyone on a street must join in, no matter what | Yes, she was letting her whole street down | No, but only because she is a girl
exercise: When Sam asked «why do the streets even fight?», what happened? | Maddie realised there was no real reason | Maddie gave him a list of reasons | Maddie told him to mind his business | Maddie said Station Road started it in 1990
exercise: Which is true about people on another street? | They are not better or worse because of where they live | They are rougher than kids on your street | They are always the ones who start the trouble first | They should not be allowed to use your shops
exercise: What would help end the Mill Lane and Station Road feud? | Giving the ball back and saying sorry | Painting a bigger sign on the other wall | Taking their goalposts too, to make it even | Waiting for them to give up
exercise: What does «feud» mean? | A long quarrel between groups that keeps going | A single fight that ends quickly | A football match between two streets every year | A party for two streets
test: Kieran: «Oi Sam, u're lookout tonight, just whistle if a grown-up comes». Best reply? | No, I'm not being part of it | OK but I won't whistle too loud | OK, if Maddie's coming too then I'll come | Fine, what time
test: «Station Road lot are scum, everyone knows». Is that true? | No, people aren't bad because of their street | Yes, everyone says so, so it must be true | Only the older ones who hang round the shop | Only the ones near the shop
test: Mate: «it's only paint lol, rain'll wash it off». True? | No, it's vandalism and costs money to clean | Yes, paint always washes off in the rain | Yes, bus stops are meant for painting on | Only if it's not red
test: «finders keepers, the ball came over our fence». What's right? | Give it back to them | Keep it, they're from Station Road | Sell it at school for a quid | Hide it till they forget about it
test: Jake's at the bus stop and both streets shout «traitor!». What's true about Jake? | He's done nothing wrong; it's not about him | He has to choose a side by Friday or he's out | He's a traitor to both sides | He should fight both sides
test: Maddie's new swimming mate is from Station Road. Kieran: «u can't hang with HER». Wise answer? | She's my mate, the street thing is daft | OK, I'll stop talking to her from now on | I'll ask her to switch streets | I'll pretend I don't know her when they're about
test: Mum at Christmas: «you two, budge up and pass the gravy». Kieran won't look at his cousin. What's sensible? | Be normal with his cousin; it's family | Sit on the stairs instead of at the table | Say Station Road started it all | Make the cousin pass him everything at dinner
test: «if u don't join the gang ur not Mill Lane». Lily says? | I'm Mill Lane and I'm not joining | Fine, I'll join for a bit and see what it's like | I'll ask my friend to join too | I'll move to Station Road then
test: Teacher: «Can anyone tell me why your streets are fighting?» Long silence. What does that show? | Nobody has a real reason | It's a secret only big kids know | The teacher should already know | The other street started it
test: «just go round and get our ball back off them, smash a window if u have to». Wise move? | Ask an adult to help talk to them | Smash just one window so they know | Take one of their bikes in swap for the ball | Go at night so nobody sees
test: Sam: «why do we even fight them?» Kieran: «cos». What's true? | «Cos» isn't a reason; it's worth asking | «Cos» is a good enough reason | Sam is being disloyal | Sam should go and fight them to find out
exam: Identify the principal characteristic of a feud between neighbouring streets as described. | It persists long after any original cause has been forgotten | It is invariably caused by a serious offence committed by one side | It is resolved by one decisive confrontation | It is confined to children of the same age
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the application of slogans to a public bus shelter without permission? | It constitutes vandalism, the cost of removal being borne by the public | It constitutes lawful decoration of public property by local residents | It is permissible if the paint is of a washable kind | It is without consequence if no person observes it
exam: It is given that a child is requested to keep watch during a planned altercation. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The child should decline and inform a trusted adult, a lookout being a participant | The child may keep watch provided he strikes no blow himself | The child may keep watch if the altercation is between other streets entirely | The child should keep watch and report afterwards
exam: State the conclusion most consistent with the observation that friends and relatives reside in the opposing street. | Residence in a particular street determines neither the worth nor the hostility of a person | Friendships with residents of the other street ought to be ended | Relatives residing in the opposing street are to be regarded as opponents | Residence in a particular street determines a person's loyalty and character entirely
